[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: The patched tree-sitter grammar cannot lex a dot in an unquoted choice option

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-TREE-SITTER-CHOICE-OPTION-CANNOT-LEX-A-DOT
TITLE: The patched tree-sitter grammar cannot lex a dot in an unquoted choice option
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
overlays/generic/tree-sitter-strictdoc.patch now adds the QUOTED arm to
choice_option, which is the only way to write an option containing a
parenthesis and which costs ZERO regressions on strictdoc's own docs. The
UNQUOTED arm is still upstream's `[\w/-]+`, so an option containing a `.` --
`GPL-3.0-or-later`, any SPDX identifier with a version -- does not parse, even
though strictdoc itself accepts it (`(["])[^,]+\1|[^,()"]+`).

Widening the charset was measured three ways and every one lost, all on
strictdoc_04_release_notes.sdoc against a 9-error-node baseline:

  transcribing strictdoc's own classes  -> 89 nodes, 76795 -> 98406 error bytes
  upstream's charset plus a bare `.`    -> 89 nodes
  a dot only BETWEEN word runs          -> 108 nodes

The error BYTES rise with the node count, so this is a real loss of coverage,
not error-recovery reshaping. Cause: tree-sitter lexes by longest match over
every token valid in the current state -- and in an error-recovery state every
token is valid -- so a wider choice_option out-competes the narrow tokens
wherever recovery is running.

Fix shape: restrict WHERE the token can be lexed rather than widening WHAT it
matches -- an external scanner, or restructuring grammar_field_single_choice /
_multiple_choice so the option list is lexed in a state of its own. Belongs to
SLICE-SDOC-IN-SEMBLE, which owns the grammar patch.

Until then packages/strictdoc-grammar/fixtures/foreign.sgra carries
`GPL-3-only`, not `GPL-3.0-or-later`; the dot was incidental to what that
fixture exercises and its README says so.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Found by checks/strictdoc-grammar-corpus.nix the moment foreign.sgra entered the
repository's own corpus -- the check did exactly its job. Recorded rather than
fixed because a charset is the wrong lever and the right one is a grammar
restructure in another slice.
<<<
